WEBVTT

1
00:00:08.669 --> 00:00:13.352
Dinner was over, but Quinn was still sitting at the kitchen table, talking to Mom and Grandpa.

2
00:00:13.372 --> 00:00:17.635
"What was the most boring thing that happened at school today?"

3
00:00:17.995 --> 00:00:19.176
Grandpa asked teasingly.

4
00:00:20.277 --> 00:00:22.459
Quinn was eager to complain about math class.

5
00:00:23.079 --> 00:00:29.384
"My teacher gave us the most boring assignment ever—Statistics and You.

6
00:00:30.828 --> 00:00:36.092
"We have to use two different kinds of graphs to illustrate statistics that affect our lives.

7
00:00:37.493 --> 00:00:40.655
I don't know any statistics, never mind some that affect me.

8
00:00:41.896 --> 00:00:42.976
Where do I start?"

9
00:00:42.996 --> 00:00:47.840
"You could start right here," Grandpa suggested.

10
00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:50.722
"Why am I having dinner with you?"

11
00:00:50.742 --> 00:00:57.446
"Because you're staying with us temporarily after falling and breaking your wrist."

12
00:00:58.827 --> 00:00:59.688
Grandpa nodded.

13
00:01:01.364 --> 00:01:07.328
"Now take out your phone and look up some statistics on older Americans and falls."

14
00:01:08.889 --> 00:01:11.870
Quinn quickly found an article online and read part of it aloud.

15
00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:18.294
"'Every year one in four Americans aged 65 and over falls.

16
00:01:20.155 --> 00:01:25.038
These 29 million falls lead to 27 thousand deaths.'

17
00:01:26.139 --> 00:01:30.409
That's a lot of falls, but how do these statistics affect me?

18
00:01:30.769 --> 00:01:31.709
I'm not over 65."

19
00:01:31.749 --> 00:01:35.551
"Where are you sleeping tonight?"

20
00:01:36.011 --> 00:01:36.751
Grandpa asked.

21
00:01:36.771 --> 00:01:42.414
"On the living room couch because you're sleeping in my bed Oh, I get it!

22
00:01:44.375 --> 00:01:47.436
You're one of those older Americans who fell.

23
00:01:48.096 --> 00:01:52.718
The statistics are about seniors, but their problems affect their families too."

24
00:01:52.738 --> 00:01:57.600
"Exactly." Grandpa turned to Mom.

25
00:01:58.764 --> 00:02:03.707
"When my wrist heals, I should take a class at your fitness center to improve my balance."

26
00:02:04.667 --> 00:02:08.750
"That's a great idea," she said, "if we're still open.

27
00:02:10.431 --> 00:02:14.052
People used to exercise when they got off work at the candy factory.

28
00:02:15.253 --> 00:02:19.215
But business has declined since the factory shut down two months ago."

29
00:02:20.616 --> 00:02:25.459
Quinn realized that her life would definitely be affected if Mom's fitness center went out of business.

30
00:02:25.479 --> 00:02:30.661
"Hey, Mom, do you keep track of how many clients work out every day?"

31
00:02:30.681 --> 00:02:33.343
"Yes," Mom said.

32
00:02:33.363 --> 00:02:39.866
"I just got an idea for my math project, but I need to know how many people have

33
00:02:39.986 --> 00:02:42.047
used the center in the past six months."

34
00:02:44.348 --> 00:02:47.989
A few nights later, Quinn delivered a presentation at the kitchen table.

35
00:02:48.009 --> 00:02:53.132
"This is a single-line graph," she announced, holding up a paper.

36
00:02:54.568 --> 00:02:59.292
"It shows the number of people who have used the fitness center in the past six months.

37
00:03:00.193 --> 00:03:03.336
The figures are fairly constant for the first four months.

38
00:03:04.197 --> 00:03:08.681
But once the factory closes, they drop by 23 percent."

39
00:03:10.062 --> 00:03:11.143
Mom looked worried.

40
00:03:11.163 --> 00:03:15.507
"And I know new memberships are down too!"

41
00:03:15.527 --> 00:03:21.032
"But there's also some promising news," Quinn said.

42
00:03:22.156 --> 00:03:24.457
"The number of older Americans is growing.

43
00:03:26.837 --> 00:03:31.239
That's partly due to improved medical care, which has boosted life expectancy.

44
00:03:32.639 --> 00:03:38.901
Only 42 percent of babies born in 1900 could expect to live past 65.

45
00:03:39.401 --> 00:03:44.562
But for people born in 2000, that number is projected to almost double."

46
00:03:44.582 --> 00:03:50.284
"So there are more old people now," Grandpa said.

47
00:03:51.284 --> 00:03:52.544
"How is that good news?"

48
00:03:54.165 --> 00:03:56.586
"Compare these two pie charts," Quinn said.

49
00:03:56.606 --> 00:03:57.826
"The U.S.

50
00:03:57.886 --> 00:04:04.508
Census Bureau collects and compiles lots of statistics, so I looked up some census data for our town.

51
00:04:05.708 --> 00:04:11.770
Like the rest of the U.S., people over 65 made up around 12 percent of the population in

52
00:04:11.930 --> 00:04:19.872
2000. But now that number has increased to 15 percent, and it's expected to grow even more."

53
00:04:21.572 --> 00:04:25.955
"Maybe I should refocus my business for older members," Mom said.

54
00:04:25.975 --> 00:04:32.818
"Besides working on balance, I could offer strength-training, stretching, and aerobic exercises."

55
00:04:32.838 --> 00:04:37.381
"You could conduct a survey to see if people are interested!"

56
00:04:37.661 --> 00:04:38.481
Quinn suggested.

57
00:04:38.501 --> 00:04:43.284
"Oh, seniors are definitely interested," Grandpa said.

58
00:04:43.304 --> 00:04:50.788
"My friend Jim says his walking group can't meet during winter because the park's paths get icy.

59
00:04:52.072 --> 00:04:55.374
So his group would love senior fitness classes too."

60
00:04:55.394 --> 00:05:01.918
"That's what my math teacher calls anecdotal evidence, Grandpa," Quinn said.

61
00:05:01.938 --> 00:05:07.001
"You're drawing conclusions based only on your opinion—and Jim's."

62
00:05:08.682 --> 00:05:10.323
Grandpa pretended to be angry.

63
00:05:10.343 --> 00:05:14.025
"I've known Jim since high school!

64
00:05:14.685 --> 00:05:16.646
We're really smart guys!"

65
00:05:17.947 --> 00:05:18.667
Quinn laughed.

66
00:05:21.032 --> 00:05:25.254
"Yes, but you two are from the same segment of the population.

67
00:05:26.415 --> 00:05:29.096
You're the exact same age, and you're both men.

68
00:05:29.116 --> 00:05:36.941
It would be better to hear from a wider demographic through a random survey including people of different

69
00:05:37.061 --> 00:05:37.961
ages and genders.

70
00:05:39.142 --> 00:05:42.984
This would give Mom a more representative sample of the senior community."

71
00:05:43.004 --> 00:05:50.548
"I could draw up the questions," Mom said, "but how will we obtain a representative sample?"

72
00:05:51.764 --> 00:05:53.665
"That's easy," Grandpa said.

73
00:05:53.685 --> 00:06:00.510
"Everyone eats at the Supreme Diner on Tuesdays because it's Senior Discount Day.

74
00:06:02.691 --> 00:06:08.395
The diner's next door to your fitness center, so I could survey people passing by.

75
00:06:09.816 --> 00:06:16.201
Plus there are seniors of all ages and genders—that's a wider demographic."

76
00:06:17.581 --> 00:06:18.282
Quinn nodded.

77
00:06:18.302 --> 00:06:22.878
"After you've gathered the data, I could help Mom interpret it.

78
00:06:24.319 --> 00:06:30.825
And if my teacher gives me extra credit for that, statistics could have a positive effect on my

79
00:06:30.985 --> 00:06:31.666
math grade!"
